europe's borders whether it's traveling  through them or sharing information  across them is the big theme of this  week's meeting of justice and home  affairs ministers in brussels  the ministers are set to adopt decisions  bringing the powerful schengen database  sys2 into operation on april 9.  the computer allows border authorities  and police to share information on  people and lost or stolen goods it will  be able to use biometrics and have new  alert systems while better protecting  personal data ministers will also  examine the state of play concerning  bulgaria and romania's entry into the  schengen passport-free area  the european commission is set to unveil  to ministers its smart borders package  which will speed the entry of business  travelers researchers and students who  live outside europe  the system will record the data of all  foreign nationals when they enter and  leave  in the wake of the conflict in mali and  the hostage crisis in algeria the eu's  counter terrorism coordinator jill de  kirkhof will brief the ministers on the  extent of the threat and propose ways to  tackle this growing security problem in  northern africa the first message for  the minister of interior is to make sure  that they will make their resource  available that they have they are ready  to  send good police officers good  prosecutors experts on borders  experts on prevention of radicalization  to the different initiatives which are  launched by their colleagues minister  foreign affairs in mali not only in mali  in the whole region  on friday justice ministers will discuss  a proposal for a general framework to  protect data ensuring that this security  safety net keeps up with globalization  and the rapid advances of technology  they will also exchange views on better  ways to prevent counterfeiting of the  euro and other currencies and confirm a  new text on improving the protection of  victims of domestic violence
 

